  <h2 id="demand">Why pharmacy demand is genuinely growing, not just a sales line</h2>

  <p>
    Career articles say "huge scope" about almost every field. For pharmacy in India specifically, there is a concrete reason the industry-facing paths are hiring faster than retail: export and manufacturing scale.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What is actually driving demand</div>
      <ul>
        <li>India's pharmaceutical exports were valued at roughly USD 30.5 billion in 2024-25, up from about USD 1.9 billion in 2000-01, and are expected to keep climbing.</li>
        <li>India remains the largest global supplier of generic medicines, manufacturing around 60,000 generic brands and supplying roughly 20% of global generic-drug volume.</li>
        <li>Government initiatives aimed at biologics, biosimilars, and expanded clinical-trial infrastructure are creating new categories of QA/QC, regulatory, and research roles beyond traditional generics manufacturing.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What this does not automatically mean</div>
      <ul>
        <li>It does not mean every path grows equally — retail pharmacy hiring tracks population and healthcare-access growth, not export volume.</li>
        <li>It does not mean every graduate lands an export-facing role immediately — international filing and audit experience is usually built over 3-5 years, not from day one.</li>
        <li>It does not remove the need for PCI registration or genuine subject depth — export-facing employers audit documentation and quality rigorously.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    In plain terms: if your goal is steady, patient-facing work with a lower but stable ceiling, retail or hospital pharmacy is a reasonable, real option. If your goal is a rising income ceiling tied to India's pharmaceutical export growth, QA/QC, regulatory affairs, and pharmacovigilance are the paths actually absorbing that growth right now.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Either way, the degree alone rarely moves your ceiling — a targeted skill or certification does.</strong> GMP/audit exposure for QA/QC, international submission experience for regulatory affairs, or a pharmacovigilance certification each function as a high-income skill layered on top of PCI registration, and that layered skill portfolio is what unlocks higher pay faster than years of tenure alone.
  </p>

